# cp.pdxdoc — long-form doc for `doc cp`
#
# Wave: R50  Milestone: M5 (1.0 signed release)
# Doc format: `design/tooling/plan.md` I7 §2 in the paideia-os repo.
# Rendered by the `doc` tool (Category F, P0) — parses this file per
# `doc.M1-002 .pdxdoc file-format parser` and emits a paginated,
# cross-referenced text render to KIND_TTY.
#
# The .pdxdoc format is line-oriented: `@section`, `@subsection`,
# `@para`, `@example`, `@xref`, and `@posix-diff` directives are
# recognised by the doc parser at doc.M1-002; every other line is
# body text within the current section. Ordering is significant —
# the parser walks the file top-to-bottom to build the render tree.

@doc-format-version 1
@tool cp
@version 1.0.0
@one-liner Copy a file or a directory tree, with per-file cap re-signing.

@section 1. Name

@para
cp — copy a file or a directory tree, with per-file capability
re-signing. cp is WIRED for single-invocation transactional
atomicity and a PdxFS undo record on overwrite, but neither
guarantee is real yet — see §3 for exactly which kernel syscalls are
still missing.

@section 2. Synopsis

@example
cp [--flag ...] [-flag ...] <src> <dst>
cp -r <src-dir> <dst-dir>

@para
Two positional arguments — source path and destination path — are
required. When `-r` is present, the source is a directory tree and
the destination is the parent directory of the target tree. When
`-r` is absent the source must be a regular file.

@section 3. Description

@para
cp wraps the whole invocation (single-file, or every entry of a
`cp -r` tree) in one `pdxfs_txn_begin` / `pdxfs_txn_commit` /
`pdxfs_txn_abort` triple, and every failure inside the copy or walk
body routes through the abort call before cp exits. This is the
INTENDED atomicity design — the wiring is real — but as of this
writing the paideia-os kernel exposes only `sys_pdxfs_txn_open`
(sysno 70); no commit/abort syscall ordinal is exposed yet, so all
three trampolines are still no-op stubs (`pdxfs_txn_begin` /
`_commit` return 0 unconditionally; `pdxfs_txn_abort` likewise).
**The atomicity guarantee is therefore not real yet**: a mid-copy
crash today leaves whatever bytes had already been written, exactly
like a copy with no transaction at all. This is a tracked paideia-os
kernel dependency (not a cp defect) — cp is a body-edit away from the
real guarantee the day `sys_pdxfs_txn_commit` / `_abort` land.

@para
Every cp invocation also (a) journals a `CopyRecord` to the audit
log before writing any bytes (D3 audit-first — the record is
present even on failure), (b) emits `CopyProgressRecord[]` on its
stdout fd bound to schema `CopyProgressRecord@0.1` (D2 semantic
pipes — downstream `query`, `sort`, `pipeviz` consume the typed
records without column-slicing text) for every file copied,
whether via a plain `cp` or via `cp -r`'s per-entry walk, and
(c) attempts to re-sign the destination inode's cap tail under
the invoker's `user_sk` so ownership provenance survives the copy
(`design/user/model.md` §10.2 signed-inode field). When the
invoker's key is locked or the copy crosses user boundaries, the
re-sign gracefully degrades; the file bytes still land and
`--verbose` emits a one-line diagnostic naming the degrade cause.

@para
cp always opens the destination `O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_TRUNC` and
overwrites an existing destination unconditionally — this matches
POSIX cp's default-overwrite behavior; cp does NOT refuse an
existing destination. `--over-existing` does not gate the write;
it additionally attempts to journal a PdxFS v1 undo record of the
pre-copy destination bytes before the overwrite so `undo cp
<same-args>` can restore them. As of this writing the kernel-side
`sys_pdxfs_undo_write` syscall the journal write needs is not yet
exposed (tracked as a paideia-os dependency, not a cp defect), so
`--over-existing` currently gates correctly (it detects whether the
destination exists) but does not yet persist a real undo record —
`undo cp <same-args>` will not restore anything until that syscall
lands.

@section 4. Options

@subsection 4.1 cp-specific flags

@example
-r                    Recursively copy a directory tree.
-v                    Additional stderr diagnostics only (per-directory
                      enter notice, cap-tail degrade cause). Does NOT
                      gate CopyProgressRecord[] emission — every copied
                      file emits a record regardless of -v.
--dry-run             Exit before opening the transaction or touching
                      src/dst at all; prints one confirmation line.
                      Does not enumerate the files/bytes that would be
                      copied (no dry walk is performed).
--over-existing       Attempt an undo-record write before overwriting
                      an existing destination (see §3 for the current
                      substrate-blocked state of the actual journal
                      write).

@subsection 4.2 Standard flag vocabulary (I3)

@example
--help                Render this document via `doc cp`.
--version             Parsed by libpdx-argv's standard vocabulary; no
                      version string is compiled into cp yet, so this
                      flag has no observable effect at 1.0.x.
--json                Parsed; no JSON-emission code path exists yet.
--schema              Parsed; no schema-print code path exists yet.
--verbose             Same flag as `-v` above (short/long alias fold
                      into one mirror bit).
--quiet               Parsed into ParsedArgs; cp never reads it back —
                      `--quiet` currently has no effect.
--no-cap:<name>       Drop the named cap before exec (per D5).
--pdx-schema          Set emit_schema=1 in ParsedArgs.

@section 5. Exit codes

@example
0    success (including a --dry-run invocation and a fully-drained
     `cp -r`).
1    operation failed: open/read/write error, short write, mkdir
     failure, readdir failure, source == destination (or destination
     nested under source), or the recursive worklist filling up
     (bounded at 128 pending subdirectories at once).
2    usage error (parse fail, wrong positional count).
3    reserved; no code path returns it as of this writing.
4    cap denied — the elevate broker refused (or was unreachable) when
     cp retried a destination-parent open after the first attempt
     failed.

@para
Code 3 was a bootstrap-era reservation for "argv shape accepted,
milestone body not yet wired". Every flag shape cp accepts now has a
real body (including `-r`, which lands a full directory-tree walk —
see §3), so no code path assigns exit 3 today; it remains reserved
for a future not-yet-implemented flag rather than removed outright.

@section 6. Capabilities required

@para
Every cp invocation receives these caps from the loader's InitCap
sidecar per `caps.decl`. The loader narrows the two file caps'
scope to the argument path prefixes at exec — cp cannot read or
write outside the source and destination-parent subtrees named on
argv.

@example
KIND_USER              (self)
KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT      (invoke)
KIND_PDXFS_FILE        (read,  <src>)
KIND_PDXFS_FILE        (write, <dst-parent>)
KIND_PDXFS_TXN         (invoke)

@para
cp retries via a KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL request on EVERY destination
open failure (M3 does not yet distinguish "cross-subtree" from any
other open-failure cause). The request specifies the widened
`KIND_PDXFS_FILE (write, <dst-parent>)` cap. libpdx-elevate.M1 has no
broker daemon running yet, so at 1.0.x this retry is always refused
— every elevate attempt today ends in `cp: elevate refused
(dst-parent out of scope)` and exit 4, whether the real cause would
have been "refused" or "broker unreachable"; the two are not yet
distinguishable at cp's level.

@section 7. Output schemas

@para
cp binds fd 1 (stdout) to `CopyProgressRecord@0.1` at
`Main::cp_main` before dispatching. Every successful per-file copy —
single-file or one entry of a `cp -r` walk — emits one record: source
path, destination path, bytes_copied, and bytes_total (bytes_total
equals bytes_copied at 1.0.x; a distinct total arrives once
`pdxfs_stat` is read before the copy starts, not only for
`--over-existing`). `elapsed_ns` is present in the record shape but
is a hardcoded 0 at 1.0.x — wall-clock timing is not wired yet. There
is no cap-tail-preserve-outcome field in the record; the preserve
outcome is only ever visible via a `--verbose` stderr line, never on
the structured stdout stream. Downstream:

@example
$ cp -r /src/tree /dst/tree | query 'select path where bytes > 1M'
$ cp -r /src/tree /dst/tree | pipeviz --bar bytes

@para
The record schema hash is stable across every 1.0.x release. A
schema-incompatible bump is a 2.0 concern; the M3-001 `--schema`
flag prints the current schema definition on stdout for offline
inspection.

@section 8. Audit + undo

@para
Every cp invocation writes an audit record via libpdx-audit before
it writes any bytes (`audit_begin`) and seals it with the exit code
at commit time (`audit_commit`) — the audit log names the attempt
even when nothing landed. The "partial state rolled back at the TXN
boundary" part of that seal is aspirational until the kernel-side
TXN commit/abort syscalls land (see §3) — today the TXN wrap runs
but the rollback it wraps around is a no-op.

@para
`--over-existing` correctly detects (via a real `pdxfs_stat` call,
per §3) whether the destination already exists, but the PdxFS v1
undo-journal WRITE itself (`sys_pdxfs_undo_write`) is not yet exposed
by the kernel — this is the one piece of the M2-005 design that
remains a stub. `undo cp <same-args>` therefore has nothing to
replay yet: no undo record is actually persisted. Multi-file
recursive copies will write one undo record per over-written
destination once the syscall lands; `undo` will then replay them in
reverse order.

@section 9. POSIX differences

@posix-diff
None here at 1.0.x — paideia-os cp overwrites an existing
destination unconditionally, same as POSIX cp's default. (An earlier
draft of this document claimed paideia-os cp refuses an existing
destination without `--over-existing`; no code path has ever done
that — see §3.)

@posix-diff
POSIX cp emits no output on success; paideia-os cp emits
CopyProgressRecord[] on stdout (empty when the source is empty).
`--quiet` is parsed but not yet wired to suppress them — a
POSIX-shell script expecting silence on cp's stdout should redirect
it explicitly until `--quiet` lands.

@posix-diff
POSIX cp does not journal to an audit log; paideia-os cp writes
a CopyRecord before every operation (audit-first per D3).

@posix-diff
POSIX cp treats every copy as opaque bytes; paideia-os cp attempts
cap-tail re-signing at every destination so ownership provenance
travels with the file.

@posix-diff
POSIX cp exit codes are 0 / >0; paideia-os cp distinguishes usage
(2) and cap-denied (4) so scripts can branch on the cause. Exit 3 is
reserved (see §5) but not returned by any code path at 1.0.x.

@section 10. Examples

@example
# Single file, silent success.
$ cp /home/alice/notes.md /tmp/notes.md
$ echo $?
0

@example
# Recursive tree copy with progress records piped to a filter.
$ cp -r /src/photos /backup/photos | query 'where bytes > 4M'

@example
# Overwrite an existing destination. --over-existing is accepted and
# correctly detects the pre-existing file, but does not yet persist a
# real undo record (sys_pdxfs_undo_write is not exposed — see §8), so
# `undo cp` has nothing to replay at 1.0.x.
$ cp --over-existing /new/config /etc/paideia/config
$ echo $?
0

@example
# Preview: guarantees no bytes move, but does not enumerate files.
$ cp --dry-run -r /src /dst
cp: dry run: no changes made
$ echo $?
0

@example
# Refused: destination nested under source (cp.ENH-008).
$ cp -r /src /src/sub
cp: source and destination refer to the same path
$ echo $?
1

@example
# Cross-subtree destination — elevate hop refused (no broker daemon
# running yet at 1.0.x, so every elevate attempt is currently refused).
$ cp report.pdf /srv/shared/2026/report.pdf
cp: elevate refused (dst-parent out of scope)
$ echo $?
4

@example
# Cap-tail re-sign degrades (invoker's key locked); bytes still land.
$ cp -v report.pdf /home/bob/inbox/report.pdf
cp: destination cap-tail unsigned (key locked)
$ echo $?
0

@section 11. Files

@example
caps.decl              Capability manifest (baseline cap set).
manifest.pdxproj       paideia-as build manifest.
manifest.pdxsig        Dual-signed release manifest (author + root).
CHANGELOG.md           Per-version change log.
doc/cp.pdxdoc          This document.
src/*.pdx              Source (12 modules, one responsibility each).
tests/*.md             Test-spec matrices (18 rows across 3 files).

